<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#siculo-arabic">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siculo-Arabic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sicilian Arabic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siculo-Arabic was the variety of Arabic spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> in the 9th century. It remained in use among Sicily's Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> under Norman rule but died out on the island in the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In Malta it developed into Maltese, the only Semitic language written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, and it left many words in Sicilian.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Siculo-Arabic, or Sicilian Arabic, is a group of Arabic varieties spoken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from the 9th century and persisting under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> until the 13th century. It descends from the Arabic brought to the island by the Muslim conquest of Byzantine Sicily, which was piecemeal and slow. Sicily was a frontier zone even after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> in 962 completed the invasion.</p><p>African Romance and Byzantine Greek continued in use long after the conquest. The speakers of Siculo-Arabic were largely Sicilian Muslims, although the foundation charter of the church of Santa Maria dell'Ammiraglio, written in both Greek and Arabic, suggests that it was also the mother tongue of many Orthodox Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. It is attested only in writings from the 9th to the 13th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> the island had two main non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> language groups: Arabic speakers, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a>, Butera, Enna and Noto, and Greek speakers, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a>, Cefalu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a> and Syracuse. In 1086 the Normans secured the conversion of ibn Hamud, the last important Kalbid ruler of Enna, and this, together with their adoption of many Arab ways of government, led to the emergence of a Christian Siculo-Arabic.</p><p>The Norman chancery worked in Arabic, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and its documents are among the main sources for Arabic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In 1154 the Arab geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Muhammad al-Idrisi</a> completed at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> the world map and description known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-rogeriana">Tabula Rogeriana</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufens</a> dropped Arabic as a language of government in 1198 and expelled the remaining Muslims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Lucera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 13th century, so that the only remaining speakers were Christians. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1282 and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> the only official language, Greek and Arabic official records ceased by the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Maltese is the sole surviving descendant of Siculo-Arabic. It evolved over some 800 years through a gradual Latinisation that followed the re-Christianisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, complete by 1250, and it received a strong Romance superstrate. Hardly any records survive from the 14th century, and the earliest record of Maltese is Il-Kantilena by Pietru Caxaro, from the late 15th century, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script. Present-day Sicilian, an Italo-Dalmatian language, kept relatively little from Siculo-Arabic, about 300 words, mostly relating to agriculture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siculo-Arabic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Traces of the emirate remain in modern Sicily: minor Arabic influence in the Sicilian language and in place names, and a much larger one in Maltese, which derives from <b>Siculo-Arabic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
